{
  "analysis": "The user reports they can't curl example.com. The patch history shows a prior benchmark task that involved installing curl via apt. I need to diagnose why curl fails - could be curl not installed, DNS issues, network/proxy issues, or firewall. Let me start investigating.",
  "plan": "First check if curl is installed and what happens when we try to curl. Then check DNS resolution, network connectivity, and any environment/proxy configuration. I'll run several diagnostic commands to narrow down the cause.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "which curl; echo '---'; curl -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 30\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "echo \"http_proxy=$http_proxy https_proxy=$https_proxy no_proxy=$no_proxy\"\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "curl is installed at /usr/bin/curl, no proxy is set, but curl fails with 'DNS resolution error'. This is a DNS resolution problem, not a connectivity or proxy issue. I need to investigate DNS configuration: /etc/resolv.conf, the resolver, and whether name resolution works at all.",
  "plan": "Check /etc/resolv.conf for nameservers, check if there's any resolver config, try resolving example.com with dig/host/getent, and test basic connectivity. This will tell me whether nameservers are misconfigured, missing, or if DNS traffic is blocked.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /etc/resolv.conf 2>&1; echo '--- nsswitch ---'; grep hosts /etc/nsswitch.conf 2>&1\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "getent hosts example.com; echo \"getent_rc=$?\"; echo '---'; nslookup example.com 2>&1 | head -n 10; echo '---'; host example.com 2>&1 | head\n",
      "duration": 3.0
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "Key finding: the system resolver works (getent hosts example.com returns IPv6 addresses, rc=0). The nameserver is 127.0.0.11 (Docker's embedded DNS). But curl specifically fails with 'DNS resolution error' while getent succeeds. This points to a curl-specific issue rather than a system DNS failure. Likely causes: a curl config file (~/.curlrc or /etc/curlrc) passing bad options, curl using a different resolver library, or an IPv4/IPv6 (Happy Eyeballs) interaction. I need to dig into curl's behavior.",
  "plan": "Check for curl config files that might inject bad options. Run curl with -4 and -6 to see if one family works. Capture the full verbose output. Also test curl against the embedded resolver with a public DNS to isolate whether it's curl's internal resolver that's broken.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la /etc/curlrc ~/.curlrc 2>&1; echo '---'; cat /etc/curlrc 2>/dev/null; cat ~/.curlrc 2>/dev/null\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "curl -4 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15; echo '=== IPV6 ==='; curl -6 -v example.com 2>&1 | head -n 15\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ]
}

